GraphQL lets a client select fields and traverse related objects in one operation, so the response shape follows the selection rather than a server-chosen fixed representation. Queries read, mutations change data, and subscriptions represent ongoing updates. A response has a top-level `data` key; it can also have top-level `errors`, including when some requested fields succeeded and another field failed. Therefore a client must not treat HTTP success alone as application success: it should inspect both data and errors, render usable partial data when appropriate, and surface or retry the failed part according to its policy. Use variables for dynamic values instead of string-building queries.

```graphql caption="A named query selects only the fields needed by the client."
query User($id: ID!) {
  user(id: $id) {
    id
    name
  }
}
```

A connection commonly exposes edges containing a node and an opaque cursor, plus `pageInfo` with at least `endCursor` and `hasNextPage`; `startCursor` and `hasPreviousPage` support reverse traversal. A `totalCount` can be useful, but it may be expensive or have snapshot semantics that must be documented. The client requests a bounded `first` amount and passes the previous end cursor as `after` for the next page. The schema should paginate list fields that can grow large. The UI must merge pages by stable identity, preserve the server's ordering, and handle a page that succeeds with partial errors rather than assuming every requested field is present.

```graphql caption="The next request uses the opaque end cursor returned by the previous page."
query Feed($after: ID) {
  posts(first: 20, after: $after) {
    nodes { id title }
    pageInfo { endCursor hasNextPage }
  }
}
```
